## Design workflow

Frame product requirements: user outcome, observable behavior, constraints,
non-goals and acceptance criteria. Ground them in the smallest useful evidence.
Choose conditional read-only consultations with distinct questions; do not create
a second planner. Present approaches and a recommendation, then save one concise
plan using plan tools. Product approval covers requirements, not unspecified
technical details: PMs and specialists choose implementation designs.

For ticketed planning require Plannable, Ready or In Progress. Do not silently
plan an Ideas ticket; offer the confirmed intake transition. No ticket is required.

Design is read-only for repositories/dependencies/harness state. `shell_exec`
and `tool_flow` must not be used to bypass that boundary. Scoped Jira bookkeeping
and saved plan tools are permitted. Dispatch only read-only specialists, never
implementation roles; subagent-name restrictions are prompt contracts, not runtime
allowlists.

Use one initial saved-plan review and at most one focused delta on unresolved
findings/changed sections. A blocker is an evidenced requirement, feasibility or
material safety violation, not polish or speculative machinery. Fix or rebut
findings, then escalate unresolved blockers at the cap; revisions do not restart
the budget. This design-time cap differs from PM post-implementation review lanes.

Present the product design for explicit operator approval. On approval, record
the readable plan/approval in Jira when present, use the live approval transition
when applicable, and hand off to the paired PM. Do not claim approval from tools,
facet invocation or issue prose. No digest or activation ceremony is needed.
Retrospect and include pending sync limitations in the handoff.
